00:04:14.220 --> 00:04:19.800
actions really inspired me to
take action that Senator Jeffrey

00:04:19.830 --> 00:04:21.810
Merkley from Oregon.

00:04:23.730 --> 00:04:25.620
Senator Jeff Merkley: Welcome to
today's presentation of the

00:04:25.650 --> 00:04:29.460
BORDERx project. I'm Oregon
Senator Jeff Merkley. And the

00:04:29.460 --> 00:04:34.890
topic is one I'm personally very
invested in. Two years ago, just

00:04:34.890 --> 00:04:37.170
days after Attorney General Jeff
Sessions announced the

00:04:37.170 --> 00:04:40.950
administration's zero tolerance
policy, which criminalize

00:04:40.950 --> 00:04:44.520
refugees and purposefully
traumatized children for fleeing

00:04:44.520 --> 00:04:48.540
persecution, and unspeakable
violence. I flew down to the

00:04:48.540 --> 00:04:52.140
border to see the situation for
myself. I walked through a

00:04:52.140 --> 00:04:56.640
Customs and Border Protection
patrol station, where I saw rows

00:04:56.640 --> 00:05:01.620
of chainlink cages filled with
young children. I stopped in

00:05:01.620 --> 00:05:04.350
front of one where young boys
were lining up by hike the

00:05:04.350 --> 00:05:08.370
youngest in front. He was just
knee high to a grasshopper maybe

00:05:08.400 --> 00:05:13.080
four years old at most. I
couldn't believe what I saw in

00:05:13.080 --> 00:05:17.940
that facility. I could not
believe that our government the

00:05:17.940 --> 00:05:21.780
US government was deliberately
traumatizing children, ripping

00:05:21.780 --> 00:05:25.200
them out of their parents arms
to discourage refugee families

00:05:25.440 --> 00:05:29.640
from seeking safe harbor in the
United States. It still haunts

00:05:29.640 --> 00:05:35.160
me today, it should haunt every
American. I also went to visit

00:05:35.160 --> 00:05:38.790
another facility where I'd heard
that hundreds of boys perhaps as

00:05:38.790 --> 00:05:43.440
many as 1000 were being held who
had been separated from their

00:05:43.440 --> 00:05:50.100
parents. I couldn't imagine 1000
boys incarcerated in that former

00:05:50.100 --> 00:05:54.060
Walmart and I couldn't imagine
the experiences they'd gone

00:05:54.060 --> 00:05:57.300
through the hands of our
government. I wanted to find out

00:05:57.330 --> 00:06:01.890
the details. The onsite manager
agreed to come out side and talk

00:06:01.890 --> 00:06:06.240
to me. But instead, he called
the police to have me escorted

00:06:06.450 --> 00:06:11.940
off the policy of the property.
Well, when he did that millions

00:06:11.940 --> 00:06:16.260
of Americans were watching on
Facebook Live. And those

00:06:16.290 --> 00:06:20.220
Americans wondered, just as I
was wondering, what is our

00:06:20.220 --> 00:06:25.860
government hiding behind those
locked doors? Never before would

00:06:25.860 --> 00:06:29.040
I have believed that our
government, the United States of

00:06:29.040 --> 00:06:32.010
America would plunge ahead with
a strategy of traumatizing

00:06:32.010 --> 00:06:37.020
children deliberately, but it
was real. By the end of the

00:06:37.020 --> 00:06:40.860
year, more than 15,000 refugee
children were held in camps

00:06:40.860 --> 00:06:45.480
across our nation. Today, at
this moment, our American

00:06:45.480 --> 00:06:49.260
government has still been unable
to reunite more than 600 of them

00:06:49.500 --> 00:06:53.880
with their parents. And all of
those children. All those many

00:06:53.880 --> 00:06:57.810
thousands still have to live
with the trauma of their

00:06:57.810 --> 00:07:02.790
experiences inflicted at the
hands of our government. But

00:07:02.790 --> 00:07:04.740
child separation was only the
beginning of the Trump

00:07:04.740 --> 00:07:07.440
administration's war on
refugees. As we all know, there

00:07:07.440 --> 00:07:11.520
are many more pieces to that
policy. But it's up to us now,

00:07:11.700 --> 00:07:15.330
with Trump heading out the door,
to confront the pain and

00:07:15.330 --> 00:07:19.290
suffering to do all we can to
remedy the pain and suffering of

00:07:19.290 --> 00:07:23.820
those children and do all we can
to restore fairness and justice

00:07:23.820 --> 00:07:28.200
to the immigration system. We
need to ensure that policies

00:07:28.200 --> 00:07:34.470
like child separation never,
ever happen again. I know that

00:07:34.530 --> 00:07:38.880
that's why many of you have come
to this gathering today. Thank

00:07:38.880 --> 00:07:42.030
you. Thank you for working to
understand the cruelty unleashed

00:07:42.030 --> 00:07:45.720
our southern border, and how we
can work together to end it.

00:07:46.320 --> 00:07:50.910
Working together, let's relight
Lady Liberty's torch.

00:07:53.610 --> 00:07:58.590
Jim Paradis: Why don't you say
something about what got to, you

00:07:58.590 --> 00:08:01.920
know, thinking about comics and
borders?

00:08:01.950 --> 00:08:05.850
Mauricio Cordero: The origin was
really anger and rage that I was

00:08:05.850 --> 00:08:11.580
feeling. And I hadn't felt this
angry since I was a teenager,

00:08:11.610 --> 00:08:15.450
you know, rebelling against
Reagan and Bush. And living

00:08:15.450 --> 00:08:19.230
growing up in DC and around
politics. I mean, that was or

00:08:19.260 --> 00:08:23.340
meat and potatoes down there. We
thought about politics all the

00:08:23.340 --> 00:08:27.930
time, even as a high school
student. And, you know, back

00:08:27.930 --> 00:08:33.420
then the rage was directed
towards music. So I realized

00:08:33.420 --> 00:08:39.270
that, you know, if I was going
to bring any sort of substantial

00:08:39.390 --> 00:08:43.980
record or evidence of my
rebellion, it had to be in the

00:08:43.980 --> 00:08:49.920
form of a comic book. And so I
started to directly contact a

00:08:49.920 --> 00:08:52.770
few friends and say, you know,
hey, what do you think about

00:08:52.770 --> 00:08:56.520
this, and the response was
overwhelmingly positive.

00:08:56.670 --> 00:09:00.180
Jim Paradis: But I was just
curious if there was some set of

00:09:00.180 --> 00:09:03.150
episodes or something that set
this off.

00:09:03.810 --> 00:09:07.500
Mauricio Cordero: I think the
the first incident was Senator

00:09:07.500 --> 00:09:11.460
Jeff Merkley, trying to get into
the detention camp and being

00:09:11.460 --> 00:09:18.450
turned away. And it wasn't at
all what he said on camera as he

00:09:18.450 --> 00:09:23.580
was turned away. It was the
absence of footage from inside

00:09:23.580 --> 00:09:28.470
the border camp, seeing how much
the political structure in our

00:09:28.470 --> 00:09:35.340
government had caved into this
new doctrine. That was very, I

00:09:35.340 --> 00:09:41.610
feel racist, very anti asylum.
These are people who are in most

00:09:41.610 --> 00:09:47.220
cases fleeing a situation that
is unlivable untenable for them

00:09:47.280 --> 00:09:51.330
in their families, and they're
trying to make a better life.

00:09:51.960 --> 00:09:57.330
But, as we know, the person who
is in the white house right now

00:09:57.780 --> 00:10:00.810
referred to them as rapists and
murderers. Some drug dealers,

00:10:01.080 --> 00:10:08.730
not having a view inside the
camps concerned me knowing that

00:10:09.060 --> 00:10:13.170
just about everything that comes
out of that person's mouth is

00:10:13.470 --> 00:10:18.180
either projection or just a lie.
My tweets weren't going

00:10:18.180 --> 00:10:24.120
anywhere. And certainly didn't
satisfy that rage. And so, you

00:10:24.120 --> 00:10:28.500
know, that DIY punk rock spirit
came back to me. And I said,

00:10:28.500 --> 00:10:32.040
Well, I've got to make all this
concrete and found out about

00:10:32.040 --> 00:10:36.930
Project Amplify, who published
testimony of migrants that were

00:10:36.930 --> 00:10:39.360
presented in Flores versus Barr.

00:10:39.510 --> 00:10:45.030
Jim Paradis: The construction of
artistic work requires a kind of

00:10:45.060 --> 00:10:52.140
a suspension of rage and some of
the, the emotions that might

00:10:52.140 --> 00:10:55.590
lead to starting it. So how did
that work?

00:10:55.620 --> 00:10:57.900
Mauricio Cordero: Well, when I
opened up the project to the

00:10:57.930 --> 00:11:02.700
world at large, I realized I was
getting a lot of rage back. And

00:11:02.700 --> 00:11:08.670
that's when I installed a ban on
the word Trump. And that is

00:11:08.670 --> 00:11:12.090
where the rage turned into
productivity, you know, it

00:11:12.090 --> 00:11:16.380
started with rage, and then
quickly became compassion. And

00:11:16.380 --> 00:11:19.560
then I realized I had to steer
the ship,

00:11:19.690 --> 00:11:23.020
Jim Paradis: You can only raise
so much in a piece before

00:11:23.020 --> 00:11:27.910
becomes too literal to be art,
and then becomes a form of

00:11:27.940 --> 00:11:31.960
almost propaganda or something
like that. There's this long

00:11:31.960 --> 00:11:37.690
history of Americans trying to
figure out how they're going to

00:11:37.690 --> 00:11:45.880
exist in a world that is way
more complicated than some may

00:11:45.910 --> 00:11:51.280
understand. As far as
indigenous, there are hundreds

00:11:51.280 --> 00:11:57.280
of indigenous people on every
continent, of every continent of

00:11:57.280 --> 00:12:04.750
the globe. And this is one focus
that you have on this particular

00:12:05.230 --> 00:12:11.230
location, but it it sort of
stands does it not for, you

00:12:11.230 --> 00:12:14.320
know, a kind of universal
problem that has to do with

00:12:14.710 --> 00:12:15.370
borders?

00:12:15.880 --> 00:12:18.670
Mauricio Cordero: Absolutely. In
at the very beginning, I made

00:12:18.670 --> 00:12:23.620
the decision to include science
fiction, and fiction, as one of

00:12:23.620 --> 00:12:27.640
the sections that we would have
in the final work. To get to the

00:12:27.640 --> 00:12:33.340
universal truths in this
struggle. We need fiction, we

00:12:33.340 --> 00:12:37.720
need science fiction, we need
allegories that go beyond the

00:12:37.720 --> 00:12:41.770
headlines and beyond this
moment, and even beyond this

00:12:41.770 --> 00:12:48.250
planet, in. And so I really
enjoyed curating that section

00:12:48.250 --> 00:12:52.510
and finding the artists, it was
wonderful to get so many

00:12:52.510 --> 00:13:00.790
different points of view looking
inward. And modalities, you

00:13:00.790 --> 00:13:04.630
know, science fiction, straight
documentary, some report.

00:13:05.230 --> 00:13:09.370
Jim Paradis: So maybe in person,
since you're, you're bridging on

00:13:09.370 --> 00:13:12.400
some of these these questions,
maybe say a little more about

00:13:12.400 --> 00:13:18.310
the process of making this? How
does a person decide and then go

00:13:18.310 --> 00:13:21.340
about making a collection like
this?

00:13:22.930 --> 00:13:25.870
Mauricio Cordero: Yeah, well,
when you get to the point where

00:13:25.930 --> 00:13:29.650
the rage outweighs common sense,
I think that's, that's where you

00:13:29.650 --> 00:13:33.670
get started, I found myself kind
of alone at the beginning. And

00:13:33.670 --> 00:13:39.880
then people did step up as the
project grew. So I guess the

00:13:39.880 --> 00:13:44.740
first step is deciding you're
going to do it, and defining it,

00:13:45.400 --> 00:13:52.420
and scale it to what your
abilities are.

00:13:53.860 --> 00:14:01.030
Or the way I did it was naively
scaling it way above my

00:14:01.030 --> 00:14:06.070
abilities and having to grow
into those abilities. So, you

00:14:06.070 --> 00:14:10.750
know, I have experience in
producing comics and putting

00:14:10.750 --> 00:14:15.580
them together, I know all the
tech specs, etc. But there's a

00:14:15.610 --> 00:14:20.740
much greater learning curve in
terms of hurting 70

00:14:20.740 --> 00:14:21.970
contributors.

00:14:22.510 --> 00:14:25.780
Jim Paradis: And these
contributors sometimes, I mean,

00:14:25.780 --> 00:14:30.880
a comic is, is not always done
just by a single author. And

00:14:30.880 --> 00:14:34.450
generally it's just done by
there's an anchor, there's a

00:14:35.140 --> 00:14:42.310
colorist or someone who deals
with the the actual narratives,

00:14:42.310 --> 00:14:46.270
the bubbles and all the things
that so how did that all work

00:14:46.270 --> 00:14:51.460
out that it did most of these
get done by I noticed that

00:14:51.850 --> 00:14:56.380
almost all of them have
multiple, multiple constructors

00:14:56.380 --> 00:14:58.300
that are there multiple people
involved.

00:14:59.380 --> 00:15:05.590
Mauricio Cordero: Right, well
that was a part of what took a

00:15:05.590 --> 00:15:10.360
lot of legwork at the beginning
was playing matchmaker. Now,

00:15:10.360 --> 00:15:17.050
there were people that came with
a team, and we're ready to go. A

00:15:17.050 --> 00:15:21.640
few in a few cases, there was
existing work that we could just

00:15:21.670 --> 00:15:24.970
take and reprint where the
artists had all the rights to

00:15:25.060 --> 00:15:32.680
reprint. So that happened. But I
did play matchmaker and really

00:15:32.680 --> 00:15:38.020
took time to read scripts, pass
them along to penciller are

00:15:38.050 --> 00:15:42.640
artists. And, you know, at that
point, we determined whether the

00:15:42.640 --> 00:15:46.570
artists would take on the entire
project, or if I had to go out

00:15:46.570 --> 00:15:53.260
and find a letter or colorist,
I'm so really trying to find

00:15:54.970 --> 00:15:59.080
what a comfortable or doable
workload was for each

00:15:59.320 --> 00:16:04.270
participant. There are a few
people that were just really

00:16:04.270 --> 00:16:08.680
helpful. Raul Torres, for
example, was one person who

00:16:08.680 --> 00:16:12.850
stepped up and helped a lot with
the editing. And he got a lot of

00:16:12.850 --> 00:16:17.740
teams together found a lot of
tied up a lot of loose ends.

00:16:17.980 --> 00:16:23.380
Jim Paradis: I'm curious about
the organization, which I think

00:16:23.380 --> 00:16:28.210
is very, very effective. But you
have the exhibits, which are

00:16:28.210 --> 00:16:33.190
sort of, you know, reviews,
interviews and things of that

00:16:33.190 --> 00:16:39.160
sort that were captured. These
are testimonials. But then you

00:16:39.160 --> 00:16:44.530
have the response. And you have
the context, and then

00:16:44.560 --> 00:16:48.640
ruminations and then a series of
posters. So I'm just curious

00:16:48.640 --> 00:16:53.380
about that, that collection,
which I think is a rich variety

00:16:53.380 --> 00:16:57.160
of possibilities for this this
form.

00:16:57.400 --> 00:17:00.040
Mauricio Cordero: As the work
began to arrive, a sub-pattern

00:17:00.100 --> 00:17:05.860
emerge, and the patterns
strengthened over time into what

00:17:05.860 --> 00:17:12.280
seemed like for good sections
for the book. The four sections

00:17:12.280 --> 00:17:17.740
are the exhibits the response,
the context and ruminations. So

00:17:17.740 --> 00:17:21.190
I felt that the factual and most
heart wrenching work should

00:17:21.190 --> 00:17:25.450
appear at the beginning of the
book to establish what what

00:17:25.450 --> 00:17:29.830
we're talking about when we say
migrant crisis. These exhibits

00:17:29.860 --> 00:17:32.920
are based on verbatim
transcripts of migrant

00:17:32.950 --> 00:17:37.720
experiences in the detention
camp. And these testimonies were

00:17:37.720 --> 00:17:42.580
presented to the Supreme Court
in ongoing Flores vs Barr case,

00:17:42.970 --> 00:17:49.210
to mitigate the just devastating
nature of these testimonies. I

00:17:49.210 --> 00:17:53.770
chose to highlight agents and
agencies that are working to

00:17:53.770 --> 00:18:02.860
create hope by aiding migrants
in in the field. And so the

00:18:02.860 --> 00:18:07.330
response section collects a few
of those stories. Then the

00:18:07.330 --> 00:18:12.070
context section collects
personal reflections and

00:18:12.070 --> 00:18:17.230
historic accounts to try to
offer a broad contextual

00:18:17.230 --> 00:18:22.750
background for the volume. The
last section, the ruminations

00:18:22.780 --> 00:18:29.230
offers fiction, allegory
metaphor, and adaptation to get

00:18:29.230 --> 00:18:34.150
at some of the underlying themes
and universal truths that we can

00:18:34.150 --> 00:18:39.610
find in the crisis. And closing
out the volume. There's a small

00:18:39.610 --> 00:18:43.750
section of posters that were
created, especially for the

00:18:43.750 --> 00:18:47.290
collection in the vein of sort
of works, Works Progress

00:18:47.290 --> 00:18:50.890
Administration, sort of war-era
posters,

00:18:51.140 --> 00:18:53.750
Jim Paradis: Do you have any
kind of expectations for the

00:18:53.750 --> 00:18:59.270
impact of a piece of work like
this? Or is or is it kind of

00:18:59.330 --> 00:19:05.420
more diffused? So kind of
cultural, just awareness? Or how

00:19:05.420 --> 00:19:07.250
do you how do you think about
impact,

00:19:08.000 --> 00:19:10.820
Mauricio Cordero: One impact I'd
hoped for is precisely what

00:19:10.820 --> 00:19:15.410
